  "fact_or_values": "fact",
  "verdict": "Doesn't hold up",
  "steelman": "MMR immunizations are administered in early childhood, typically between 12 and 15 months of age, which happens to be the developmental window in which symptoms of autism spectrum disorder are first recognized by parents and clinicians. A widely publicized 1998 case series in The Lancet—subsequently retracted for falsified data—formally suggested a link between the vaccine and autism, solidifying long-standing parental concerns.",
  "finding": "A Cochrane systematic review analyzing 2 observational studies with 1,194,764 children found no evidence of an association between MMR immunization and autistic spectrum disorders, observing a rate ratio of 0.93 (95% CI 0.85 to 1.01). The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention similarly reports that scientists studying the MMR vaccine across multiple countries have found no link between autism and the MMR vaccine.",
  "reasoning_flags": [
    "Correlation, not cause"
  ],
  "newest_data": "Relied on the Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews update by Di Pietrantonj et al. (published April 20, 2020, with current version November 22, 2021) and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention Measles Vaccination guidance (updated April 29, 2026). A search through October 2026 confirmed no newer Cochrane systematic review update on MMR vaccination and autism has been issued.",
  "evidence": [
